{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792228387,"nonce":"T5QYSi/YrDz5EZeNTxTnOLuqj2A=","title":"Python: widening a module-level set or dict inside a function creates an unassigned local","message":"A function that changes a module-level set or dict in place (`s.add(\"x\")` on a Set(Int)) or passes it to a parameter taking a wider set or dict, without declaring it `global`, widens the holder through Walker::widen_holder, which called `assign` on the name. The name then became a local of that body, never assigned, and the function read Undef: \"Execution error: type mismatch: expected pointer, got Undef\".\n\n    s = set([1, 2])\n    def f():\n        s.add(\"x\")\n        return len(s)\n    print(f())\n\nnqueens hit it once `set(map(frozenset, poscols))` was typed (Set(Set(Tuple))) and `place` received `r - ar` with `ar` a Set(Object).\n\nFix: such a widening is recorded in Locals::widened_globals, gathered from every item's kept inference by infer_module, and joined into the module variable's type with the writes under `global`.","files":null}]}